---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

When a thread calls timer_sleep(ticks), it first checks if ticks is 0 or negative.
If it is, it just returns, since there is nothing to wait for. Otherwise, 
it turns off interrupts and saves its wake-up time in
wakeup_tick (current tick + ticks). Then it adds itself to the
sleeping_threads list and calls thread_block(), so it stops running
and doesn't use any CPU while it sleeps.

On every tick, the timer interrupt checks sleeping_threads and
unblocks any thread whose wakeup_tick has been reached. Then it calls
thread_preempt_if_needed() so a higher-priority thread that has just
woke up can run right away.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler

The handler compares each sleeping thread's
wakeup_tick to the current tick and unblocks the ones that are ready.
It never sleeps, waits on a lock, or prints anything. Threads are
removed from the list as soon as they wake up, so the list only holds
threads that are still sleeping. The list isn't sorted, so the handler 
checks every sleeping thread each tick.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

Race conditions are avoided by turning off interrupts before
timer_sleep() touches the sleeping_threads list and keeping them off
until the thread is blocked. With keeping the interrupts off, the running thread
can't be switched out in the middle, so two threads can never add themselves 
to the list at the same time.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

Race conditions are avoided when a timer interrupt occurs during
timer_sleep() because interrupts are turned off from the moment the
thread sets its wakeup_tick until it is blocked.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

We chose this design because it gets rid of busy waiting. The
original timer_sleep() kept calling thread_yield() in a loop, which
wasted CPU time just checking the clock. In our design, the thread is
blocked while it sleeps, so it uses no CPU until the timer wakes it
up.

It is superior to the other design we considered, a list sorted by
wake-up time, because it is simpler and easier to get right. The downside is that
the timer interrupt checks every sleeping thread each tick, while a
sorted list could stop at the first thread that isn't ready yet


