>> C3: Did any ambiguities in the scheduler specification make values
>> in the table uncertain?  If so, what rule did you use to resolve
>> them?  Does this match the behavior of your scheduler?

Yes, there were ambiguities in the scheduler specification that made
values in the table uncertain. The main one is what happens when two 
or more threads have the same highest priority. I used round robin. The
thread that has been waiting the longest runs first. Another one is whether 
recent_cpu is increased before or after the priorities are recalculated 
on the same tick. We increased recent_cpu first and then recalculated 
the priority.

Yes, this matches the behavior of our scheduler. When a thread
yields, it goes to the back of the ready list, and next_thread_to_run() 
picks the first thread with the highest priority, so ties go to the 
thread that has waited longest. Also, thread_tick() increases recent_cpu 
before it recalculates priorities.

>> C4: How is the way you divided the cost of scheduling between code
>> inside and outside interrupt context likely to affect performance?

The way we divided the cost of scheduling puts most of the MLFQS work
inside the timer interrupt, because the spec says these updates have
to happen on exact ticks. Every tick, we add 1 to the running
thread's recent_cpu, which is cheap. Every 4 ticks, we recalculate
the priority of every thread. Every second, we update load_avg and
the recent_cpu of every thread. Every second, we update load_avg and
the recent_cpu of every thread. These loop over all threads, so the
interrupt gets slower as the number of threads grows.

This can affect performance because time spent in the interrupt is
counted against the thread that was running. That raises its
recent_cpu and lowers its priority, even though it didn't really use
that time.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C5: Briefly critique your design, pointing out advantages and
>> disadvantages in your design choices.  If you were to have extra
>> time to work on this part of the project, how might you choose to
>> refine or improve your design?

The main advantage is that it is simple. We use one ready list for
both the priority scheduler and MLFQS, so most of the scheduling code
is shared. When a thread's priority changes, we don't have to move it
between queues. It does pass all the MLFQS tests.

The main disadvantage is speed. Picking the next thread scans the
whole ready list, which is O(n). Using 64 queues, one per priority,
would make this close to O(1). We also recalculate the priority of
every thread every 4 ticks, even though only the running thread's
recent_cpu changes between the once-per-second updates.

If I had extra time, I would use 64 ready queues, and only
recalculate the running thread's priority every 4 ticks. I
would also remove the extra preemption check in thread_tick(), 
since timer_interrupt() already calls thread_preempt_if_needed().


>> C6: The assignment explains arithmetic for fixed-point math in
>> detail, but it leaves it open to you to implement it.  Why did you
>> decide to implement it the way you did?  If you created an
>> abstraction layer for fixed-point math, that is, an abstract data
>> type and/or a set of functions or macros to manipulate fixed-point
>> numbers, why did you do so?  If not, why not?

We decided to implement fixed-point math as a set of macros in a new
file, fixed-point.h, using the 17.14 format from the spec. We did
this so the MLFQS formulas in thread.c are easier to read, and so
every conversion uses the same scale. Writing the math out by hand
each time would be easy to get wrong.

Yes, we created an abstraction layer. It is a set of
TO_FIXED, TO_INT, ADD, SUB, MULTI, DIV, and ROUND. MULTI and DIV cast
to int64_t before multiplying, so the middle of the calculation
doesn't overflow.


